:R-HSA-9690722  a           obo:GO_0052553 , owl:NamedIndividual ;
        rdfs:comment        "This Reactome module describes modulation of IFIT proteins by diverse viral families that have evolved multiple strategies to evade IFIT-mediated restriction. Among RNA viruses, a common mechanism is acquisition of cap-1 structures, either by “cap-snatching” host mRNA fragments (e.g., in orthomyxoviruses) or through virally encoded or hijacked host 2′-O-methyltransferases, as in flaviviruses and coronaviruses (Daffis S et al., 2010; Zust R et al., 2011; reviewed by Hyde JL & Diamond MS 2015; Tsukamoto Y et al., 2024). Some viruses employ RNA secondary structures that shield the 5′ end from IFIT recognition, including the panhandle structures of bunyaviruses and arenaviruses (Hyde JL et al., 2014; Reynaud JM et al., 2015; Hankinson J et al., 2025). In addition, IFIT2 can bind adenylate–uridylate (AU)-rich elements in the 3′ UTRs of cellular and viral mRNAs, a mechanism that normally promotes translation of host interferon-stimulated genes but is co-opted by influenza virus to enhance viral protein synthesis (Yang Z et al., 2012; Tran V et al., 2020). Further, large DNA viruses such as vaccinia virus deploy proteins that directly antagonize IFIT activity (Liu R et al., 2019; Xie S et al., 2025), for example by targeting IFITs for proteasomal degradation, thereby counteracting their antiviral function (Liu R et al., 2019)." ;
        rdfs:label          "Viral strategies to evade IFIT action" ;
        dc:contributor      "Authored: Shamovsky, Veronica, 2020-05-22" , "GOC:reactome_curators" , "Reviewed: Hyde, Jennifer L, 2026-05-19" ;
        dc:date             "2026-07-02" ;
        pav:providedBy      "https://reactome.org" ;
        oboInOwl:hasDbXref  "R-HSA-9690722" ;
        skos:narrower       <http://model.geneontology.org/R-HSA-9684482/R-HSA-9684482> ;
        skos:note           "org.biopax.paxtools.model.level3.Pathway" .

